# Line-ending policy.
#
# The IUP fork is carried as a patch series applied onto a pristine upstream
# extract (see iup-fork\README.md). Upstream IUP sources are LF, so the patches
# must be LF too — `git apply` rejects a CRLF patch against LF sources with
# "patch does not apply" and the fork build fails outright.
#
# Git's Windows installer sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, so WITHOUT this
# rule every fresh clone checks the patches out as CRLF and
# scripts\build-iup-fork.ps1 breaks on a clean machine. Pin them explicitly.
*.patch text eol=lf
*.diff text eol=lf

# Scripts that run on Linux (and in the VM helpers) must stay LF, or bash reads
# "\r" into every line; PowerShell and batch files are fine either way.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf
scripts/packaging/linux/AppRun text eol=lf

# Binary data: never touch line endings. The compositor goldens (.bgra) are raw
# pixels compared byte for byte.
*.bgra binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
